        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128214; TODAY'S FOCUS</div>
        <div style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 10px;">Texas has three layers of government. Each one has jobs to do, and each one has to find the money to do them.</div>
        <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65;">In 1876, Texans wrote a constitution that kept government small and divided. You will see the government that constitution built, from the state capital down to the town hall. You will also see where the money comes from, and why so much is written into the constitution itself.</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#127919; BY THE END OF THIS LESSON YOU WILL BE ABLE TO&hellip;</div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <div style="min-width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 50%; background: #C7922C; color: #fff; font-size: 13px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">1</div>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65;"><strong>I will be able to</strong> describe the main parts of state, county, and city government in Texas.</div>
        </div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <div style="min-width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 50%; background: #C7922C; color: #fff; font-size: 13px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">2</div>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65;"><strong>I will be able to</strong> explain where state and local governments get their <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="The money a government takes in.">revenue</a>.</div>
        </div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <div style="min-width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 50%; background: #C7922C; color: #fff; font-size: 13px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">3</div>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65;"><strong>I will be able to</strong> explain why the Texas Constitution is so long, and how it changes.</div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128203; WHAT YOU NEED FOR THIS LESSON</div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 8px; border-left: 3px solid #D0D9E8; padding-left: 14px;"><span style="color: #c7922c; font-size: 18px; flex-shrink: 0;">&#9654;</span>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.6;">This page.</div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 1 &middot; THE STATE</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">The State</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The Constitution of 1876 divided state power so that no one person held too much. The governor leads the state, but voters also elect a lieutenant governor, an attorney general, a comptroller who handles the state&rsquo;s money, and a land commissioner. That is <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A way of sharing power so that each part of government can limit the others.">checks and balances</a>, built right into the offices.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The legislature has two parts. The Senate has 31 members and the House of Representatives has 150. A bill must pass both before it goes to the governor, who can sign it or veto it. The lieutenant governor leads the Senate and has a great deal of influence over which bills get a vote. The 1876 delegates did not want lawmakers meeting all the time, so the legislature meets in a regular session for 140 days every other year. The governor can call a special session of up to 30 days for a particular problem.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Texas has two top courts instead of one. The Supreme Court of Texas hears final appeals in civil cases, such as disputes over contracts or property. The Court of Criminal Appeals hears final appeals in crime cases. Judges are elected.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The delegates meant all this to keep the state government on a short leash. But a government that can barely meet cannot easily change. In 1972, voters passed an <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A change or addition to a constitution.">amendment</a> to give the governor a four-year term instead of two. It is one example of how Texans have had to adjust the 1876 plan as the state changed.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 2 &middot; COUNTIES AND CITIES</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">Counties and Cities</h3><div style="margin: 16px 0;"><div style="display: flex; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 8px;"><div style="min-width: 84px; background: #0E1C42; color: #fff; font-size: 15px; padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px; text-align: center;"><strong>State</strong></div><div style="flex: 1; background: #EEF1F8; border-left: 4px solid #0E1C42; font-size: 15px; color: #1a2340; padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px; line-height: 1.5;">Governor, lieutenant governor, attorney general, comptroller. A legislature with a Senate and a House. Courts.</div></div><div style="display: flex; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 8px;"><div style="min-width: 84px; background: #2196D0; color: #fff; font-size: 15px; padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px; text-align: center;"><strong>County</strong></div><div style="flex: 1; background: #EEF1F8; border-left: 4px solid #2196D0; font-size: 15px; color: #1a2340; padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px; line-height: 1.5;">County judge and four commissioners, sheriff, county clerk, and a tax office.</div></div><div style="display: flex; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 8px;"><div style="min-width: 84px; background: #C7922C; color: #fff; font-size: 15px; padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px; text-align: center;"><strong>City</strong></div><div style="flex: 1; background: #EEF1F8; border-left: 4px solid #C7922C; font-size: 15px; color: #1a2340; padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px; line-height: 1.5;">Mayor and city council. Police, fire, water, streets, and parks.</div></div><div style="font-size: 12px; color: #6b7a99;">Three layers of government and who runs each one.</div></div><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Most Texans deal with local government more often than with the state. Texas is divided into 254 counties, more than any other state. Many of them are small. In the 1800s, Texans wanted the county courthouse close enough to reach without a long trip. Each county has a county seat, the town where its courthouse stands.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">A county is run by a commissioners court. Despite the name, it is not a real court. It is a group of five, made up of the county judge and four commissioners, each chosen by the voters. They set the county budget and the tax rate, and they build and fix county roads. Counties also elect a sheriff, a county clerk who keeps records, and a tax assessor-collector. Counties handle jobs for the state too, such as holding elections and keeping birth and land records.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Cities are different. A city exists because the people who live there want services close together: police, fire protection, clean water, streets. Most cities have a mayor and a city council. Some hire a city manager to run daily business. A change to the constitution in 1912 gave larger cities <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="The right of a city to write its own charter, the plan for how the city is run.">home rule</a>. A city with more than 5,000 people can write its own charter and choose its own form of government, as long as it follows state law.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">There are also school districts, run by elected boards, and many special districts for things like water and hospitals. Texas has more than a thousand school districts alone. So a single Texan may be governed by a state, a county, a city, a school district, and more, all at once.</p><div class="optima-widget ow-select" data-ra-skip>
  <div class="widget-kicker">&#9889; TRY IT &middot; WHICH LAYER OF GOVERNMENT?</div>
  <div class="widget-question">Texans live under several layers of government at once. For each row, decide which layer does the job.</div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="1" data-fb="Right. This group is called the commissioners court, though it is not a real court." data-hint="Think about which layer has a county seat and a courthouse.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">1. A group of five, the county judge and four commissioners, sets the budget and builds roads.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">The state</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">The county</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">The city</button></div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="2" data-fb="Right. A change in 1912 let larger cities choose their own form of government." data-hint="Think about which layer is made of people living close together who want services like water and streets.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">2. A town with more than 5,000 people writes its own charter under home rule.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">The state</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">The county</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">The city</button></div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="0" data-fb="Right. Texas has two top courts, and both belong to the state." data-hint="Think about which layer holds the highest courts.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">3. The Court of Criminal Appeals hears the final appeals in crime cases.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">The state</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">The county</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">The city</button></div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="1" data-fb="Right. Counties also do jobs for the state, such as holding elections and keeping records." data-hint="Think about which layer has a sheriff and a county clerk.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">4. An elected clerk keeps birth and land records, and an elected sheriff keeps order.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">The state</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">The county</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">The city</button></div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="2" data-fb="Right. A city exists because people who live together want services close at hand." data-hint="Think about which layer is led by a mayor.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">5. A mayor and council run the police, the fire protection and the streets.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">The state</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">The county</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">The city</button></div>
  </div>
  <button class="widget-reset">Reset</button>
</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 3 &middot; REVENUE AND THE CONSTITUTION</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">Paying the Bills</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">All that government needs <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="The money a government takes in.">revenue</a>. Texas draws it from several sources.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The biggest local source is the <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A tax based on the value of land and buildings that a person owns.">property tax</a>. Owners of land and buildings pay it to school districts, cities, and counties. The state itself does not collect a property tax. Another source is the <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A tax added to the price of things people buy.">sales tax</a>, a small percent added to the price of what people buy. The state adopted one in 1961, and cities and counties may add a little on top. The state also takes in taxes on the oil and gas pumped from Texas ground. Texas has no personal income tax. In 2019, voters amended the constitution to say the state cannot create one without a vote of the people. That fits the old spirit of the 1876 delegates, who wanted government to stay cheap.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Governments also charge fees, for things like driver&rsquo;s licenses, toll roads, and college tuition. And they get money from the federal government, which sends grants for roads, schools, and health care.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">When a government wants to build something big, like a school or a bridge, it may borrow by selling a <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A loan to a government. The government sells bonds to people, and promises to pay the money back with interest.">bond</a>. Buyers lend the money now and are repaid later with interest. The 1876 constitution put tight limits on borrowing by the state. For city and county bonds, voters usually must approve the plan, so the people hold the purse strings.</p><h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">A Constitution That Keeps Growing</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Notice a pattern. Whenever Texans worried about too much power, they wrote a limit into the constitution itself, instead of leaving the question to the legislature. That is why the Texas Constitution is so much longer than the U.S. Constitution, and why it has been amended hundreds of times. Voters usually decide on amendments in elections held in November.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Is this good or bad? It depends on what you value. A long constitution is a strong promise that certain things will not change on a whim. It is also a heavy thing to change when times do change. Both are true, and Texas has lived with both for a long time.</p><div class="optima-widget ow-select" data-ra-skip>
  <div class="widget-kicker">&#9889; TRY IT &middot; PAYING THE BILLS</div>
  <div class="widget-question">Each row is a money problem. Pick the answer that fits what you read.</div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="2" data-fb="Right. Owners of land and buildings pay the property tax, and it is the biggest local source." data-hint="Think about a tax paid every year by people who own property in the district.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">1. A school district needs steady money every year to pay its teachers. Which source fits best?</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">A federal grant for roads</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">A bond that is repaid once</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">The property tax</button><button type="button" data-choice="3">A state income tax</button></div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="0" data-fb="Right. The city can sell a bond, borrow the money now and repay it later with interest. Voters usually must approve the plan." data-hint="Think about a way to borrow money now and repay it over time.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">2. A city wants a big new bridge it cannot pay for in a single year. What can it do?</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">Sell a bond</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">Create a personal income tax</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">Collect a fee at the courthouse door</button><button type="button" data-choice="3">Wait for the legislature to meet</button></div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="1" data-fb="Right. The state cannot create a personal income tax without a vote of the people. That fits the 1876 delegates’ wish to keep government cheap." data-hint="Think about the one big tax Texas does not have.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">3. In 2019, Texas voters changed the constitution about money. What did the change say?</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">The state must collect a property tax</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">The state cannot create a personal income tax without a vote of the people</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">The sales tax must end</button><button type="button" data-choice="3">Cities may not borrow money</button></div>
  </div>
  <button class="widget-reset">Reset</button>
</div>
    </div>

    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 12px;">&#128170; WHAT TO CARRY FORWARD</div>
        <p style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0;">Government is not only presidents and governors. It is also the county office where deeds are kept and the city council that sets a tax rate. Think about which layer of government has touched your own life this week.</p>
    </div>
</div>

